1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:16,704
This is STAT 331, video 16, part b. Part a showed that beta j hat minus beta j, over its standard error, has a t distribution with n minus p plus 1 degrees of freedom, and used it for tests.

2
00:00:17,154 --> 00:00:23,661
This part prepares you for three more question types. Each type gets a method card and worked problems.

3
00:00:24,111 --> 00:00:36,121
Type C: a confidence interval for one coefficient. Lecture 8 derives it, and Tutorial 3, question 1 h, compares a 90 and a 95 percent interval.

4
00:00:36,571 --> 00:00:50,587
Type D: the estimate, standard error and interval of a linear combination of the betas, such as beta 2 plus beta 3. Practice question 1 and Tutorial 3, question 1 d, ask this.

5
00:00:51,037 --> 00:00:57,117
Type E: the three short proofs of practice question 3, about y hat, e and X.

6
00:00:57,567 --> 00:01:14,826
Section 1: intervals. We know the distribution of T exactly, so we can find any quantile. The quantile t 1 minus alpha over 2 has area 1 minus alpha over 2 to its left. So the right tail has area alpha over 2.

7
00:01:15,276 --> 00:01:27,393
The t density is symmetric about 0. So the alpha over 2 quantile is minus t 1 minus alpha over 2. The left tail also has area alpha over 2.

8
00:01:27,843 --> 00:01:35,929
The two tails together have area alpha. So the probability that T is between the two quantiles is 1 minus alpha.

9
00:01:36,379 --> 00:01:52,528
Now solve for beta j. Line 1 is the result of the last slide. Here t means t 1 minus alpha over 2, with n minus p plus 1 degrees of freedom. The picture shows the middle area 1 minus alpha.

10
00:01:52,978 --> 00:01:56,669
Put the definition of T into the probability statement.

11
00:01:57,119 --> 00:02:13,268
Multiply all three parts by sigma hat root V j j. It is positive, so the inequalities do not change. The picture shows the half width, t times sigma hat root V j j, on each side of beta j hat.

12
00:02:13,718 --> 00:02:28,203
Multiply by minus 1. This reverses both inequalities, and the two bounds change places. They have the same size, so the statement keeps its form, with beta j minus beta j hat in the middle.

13
00:02:28,653 --> 00:02:39,384
Add beta j hat to all three parts. Now beta j is alone in the middle. The lower and upper bounds are beta j hat minus and plus the half width.

14
00:02:39,834 --> 00:02:59,375
So the interval is beta j hat plus or minus t times the standard error. It is random, because beta j hat and sigma hat change from sample to sample. Beta j is fixed. Before we see the data, the interval covers beta j with probability 1 minus alpha.

15
00:02:59,825 --> 00:03:03,665
Here is the method for question type C, in four steps.

16
00:03:04,115 --> 00:03:16,190
Step 1 of method card C: find beta j hat, its standard error, and the degrees of freedom, n minus p plus 1. Beta j hat is the center of the interval.

17
00:03:16,640 --> 00:03:30,400
Step 2: the quantile t 1 minus alpha over 2. For a 95 percent interval, it is t 0.975. The quantile times the standard error is the half width.

18
00:03:30,850 --> 00:03:37,463
Step 3: the bounds are beta j hat minus and plus the quantile times the standard error.

19
00:03:37,913 --> 00:03:52,164
Step 4: read the interval. If theta 0 is outside it, the test of beta j equals theta 0 rejects at level alpha. Interpret it in the units of y, with the other covariates fixed.

20
00:03:52,614 --> 00:04:14,587
A worked problem of type C. Pause the video and try it. Lecture 7 gives 95 percent intervals for all four coefficients. Step 1 of card C, for age: beta 1 hat is minus 1.1416, the standard error is 0.2148, and there are 42 degrees of freedom.

21
00:04:15,037 --> 00:04:24,061
Step 2: the quantile t 0.975 with 42 degrees of freedom is 2.0181.

22
00:04:24,511 --> 00:04:49,194
Step 3: 2.0181 times 0.2148 is 0.4335. So the interval is minus 1.1416 plus or minus 0.4335: from minus 1.5751 to minus 0.7081. The whole interval is below 0.

23
00:04:49,644 --> 00:05:11,660
The other intervals work the same way. The intercept: 121.9 to 195.1. Severity: minus 1.43 to 0.55. Stress: minus 27.8 to 0.86. These are the numbers of the R output on slide 51.

24
00:05:12,110 --> 00:05:30,222
Step 4: 0 is outside the intervals for the intercept and age, so the tests reject beta j equals 0. 0 is inside for severity and stress. At the same alpha, the interval leaves out theta 0 exactly when the test rejects.

25
00:05:30,672 --> 00:05:49,851
A tutorial question of type C. Pause the video and try it. Tutorial 3, question 1 h: would a 90 percent interval be narrower or wider than the 95 percent interval, and by how much? Step 1: the output gives 319 degrees of freedom.

26
00:05:50,301 --> 00:06:03,549
Step 2: for 95 percent, the quantile is t 0.975 with 319 degrees of freedom, which is 1.9674.

27
00:06:03,999 --> 00:06:15,903
For 90 percent, alpha is 0.10, so the quantile is t 0.95. It is 1.6496. It is closer to 0.

28
00:06:16,353 --> 00:06:24,054
Step 3: the width of an interval is upper minus lower. That is 2 times the quantile times the standard error.

29
00:06:24,504 --> 00:06:36,600
Divide the two widths. The 2 and the standard error cancel. 1.6496 over 1.9674 is 0.838.

30
00:06:37,050 --> 00:06:45,434
So the 90 percent interval is narrower. Its width is 0.84 times the width of the 95 percent interval.

31
00:06:45,884 --> 00:06:52,007
Section 2: linear combinations. Line 1: beta hat is multivariate normal.

32
00:06:52,457 --> 00:07:12,531
So any linear combination, a transpose beta hat, is normal too. Its mean is a transpose beta. Its variance is sigma squared, a transpose, X transpose X inverse, a. The vector a gives one weight to each coefficient. In the picture, a gives beta 2 plus beta 3.

33
00:07:12,981 --> 00:07:38,005
Replace sigma squared with sigma hat squared. The grid is the estimated variance matrix of beta hat, sigma hat squared V, for the satisfaction data. Write out a transpose V a: it is the sum of a i times a j times each cell. Only the cells with non-zero weights count: two variances in green, and the covariance two times in orange.

34
00:07:38,455 --> 00:07:46,797
Standardize as for one coefficient. The result has a t distribution with n minus p plus 1 degrees of freedom.

35
00:07:47,247 --> 00:08:00,921
So tests and intervals work as before. The standard error is the square root of sigma hat squared a transpose V a. Fitted values are also linear combinations. The next video uses this.

36
00:08:01,371 --> 00:08:05,275
Here is the method for question type D, in five steps.

37
00:08:05,725 --> 00:08:14,579
Step 1 of method card D: write the weight vector a. It has one weight for each coefficient, from beta 0 to beta p.

38
00:08:15,029 --> 00:08:21,301
Step 2: the estimate is a transpose beta hat, the sum of a j times beta j hat.

39
00:08:21,751 --> 00:08:36,812
Step 3: the variance. Use the estimated variance matrix, sigma hat squared V. Each variance on the diagonal gets the weight a i squared. Each covariance counts two times, with the weight a i a j.

40
00:08:37,262 --> 00:08:41,187
Step 4: the standard error is the square root of the variance.

41
00:08:41,637 --> 00:08:54,309
Step 5: the interval is the estimate plus or minus the t quantile times the standard error. Or test with t obs. Interpret the estimate as a change in the mean of y.

42
00:08:54,759 --> 00:09:12,829
Practice question 1 of Lecture 7 is type D. Pause the video and try it. Part a: estimate 2 beta 3. Part b: find a standard error. Part c: interpret the estimate. The grid is the estimated variance matrix of the satisfaction model.

43
00:09:13,279 --> 00:09:18,121
Step 1 of card D: the weight vector a is 0, 0, 0, 2.

44
00:09:18,571 --> 00:09:30,881
Step 2: 2 times beta 3 hat is 2 times minus 13.4702, which is minus 26.9403. This answers part a.

45
00:09:31,331 --> 00:09:44,387
Step 3: only the cell 3 3 has two non-zero weights. Its weight is 2 squared, which is 4. 4 times 50.4052 is 201.62.

46
00:09:44,837 --> 00:09:59,514
Step 4: the standard error is the square root of 201.62, which is 14.1993. This is 2 times the standard error of beta 3 hat. This answers part b.

47
00:09:59,964 --> 00:10:21,703
Part c, step 5. The picture shows the mean satisfaction against stress, with age and severity fixed. One more unit of stress changes the mean by beta 3. Two more units change it by 2 beta 3. So for two more units of stress, the estimated mean satisfaction decreases by 26.94.

48
00:10:22,153 --> 00:10:28,467
Part d: repeat parts a to c for beta 2 plus beta 3. Pause the video and try it.

49
00:10:28,917 --> 00:10:33,675
Step 1 of card D: the weight vector a is 0, 0, 1, 1.

50
00:10:34,125 --> 00:10:46,519
Step 2: add the two estimates. Minus 0.4420 plus minus 13.4702 is minus 13.9122.

51
00:10:46,969 --> 00:11:00,601
Step 3: the weights select four cells: the variance of beta 2 hat, the variance of beta 3 hat, and their covariance two times. The covariance is not 0, so it must be in the sum.

52
00:11:01,051 --> 00:11:15,131
From the grid: 0.2420 plus 50.4052 plus 2 times minus 1.7916 is 47.0640.

53
00:11:15,581 --> 00:11:25,395
Step 4: the standard error is the square root of 47.0640, which is 6.8603.

54
00:11:25,845 --> 00:11:39,818
Part c: beta 2 plus beta 3 is the change in mean satisfaction when severity and stress both increase by one unit, with age fixed. The estimate is a decrease of 13.91.

55
00:11:40,268 --> 00:12:02,305
A tutorial question of type D. Pause the video and try it. Tutorial 3, question 1 d, uses a model of teacher salary on the size of the school district. Beta 1 is the coefficient of size f 2, and beta 2 of size f 3. Video 19 explains these indicator covariates.

56
00:12:02,755 --> 00:12:09,817
Step 1 of card D: beta 2 minus beta 1 has the weight vector 0, minus 1, 1.

57
00:12:10,267 --> 00:12:21,296
Step 2: the estimate is 6035.2 minus 3527.1, which is 2508.1.

58
00:12:21,746 --> 00:12:54,081
Step 3: the R command v c o v gives the estimated variance matrix, sigma hat squared V. The weights select the two variances, 136994.86 and 241977.18, and the covariance 32012.55 two times, with the weight minus 1 times 1. The sum is 314946.94.

59
00:12:54,531 --> 00:13:00,014
Step 4: the standard error is the square root, 561.20.

60
00:13:00,464 --> 00:13:14,885
Step 5: the output gives 322 degrees of freedom. The quantile t 0.975 with 322 degrees of freedom is 1.9674.

61
00:13:15,335 --> 00:13:26,749
1.9674 times 561.20 is 1104.1. This is the half width of the interval.

62
00:13:27,199 --> 00:13:51,625
The interval is 2508.1 minus and plus 1104.1: from 1404.0 to 3612.2. The posted solution uses the unrounded estimate and gets 1403.96 to 3612.13.

63
00:13:52,075 --> 00:14:14,241
The interpretation, from the solution: beta 2 minus beta 1 is the difference in mean salary between districts of size 3 and size 2. The estimate is 2508 dollars, with a 95 percent interval from 1404 to 3612 dollars.

64
00:14:14,691 --> 00:14:27,576
Section 3: proofs. Question type E asks you to show an identity of the fitted values, the residuals and X, as in practice question 3. Method card E has four steps.

65
00:14:28,026 --> 00:14:42,746
Step 1: start from the normal equations, X transpose X beta hat equals X transpose y. They give X transpose e equals 0. In the picture, each column of X times e is 0.

66
00:14:43,196 --> 00:14:50,492
Step 2: write the expression with y hat equals X beta hat, and e equals y minus y hat.

67
00:14:50,942 --> 00:14:59,411
Step 3: use the rule that the transpose of A B is B transpose A transpose. It puts X transpose next to e.

68
00:14:59,861 --> 00:15:03,189
Step 4: put in X transpose e equals 0.

69
00:15:03,639 --> 00:15:21,303
Practice question 3 has three proofs of type E. Pause the video and try them. Part a: show that the sum of y i hat times e i is 0. The picture shows y, the fitted vector y hat in the column space of X, and the residual e.

70
00:15:21,753 --> 00:15:29,967
Step 1 of card E: start from the normal equations. X transpose X beta hat equals X transpose y.

71
00:15:30,417 --> 00:15:44,817
Put e equals y minus X beta hat into X transpose e. By line 1, the two terms are equal, so X transpose e is 0. In the picture, e is perpendicular to every column of X.

72
00:15:45,267 --> 00:15:48,147
Step 2: write y hat as X beta hat.

73
00:15:48,597 --> 00:15:57,279
Step 3: the transpose of X beta hat is beta hat transpose X transpose. Now X transpose is next to e.

74
00:15:57,729 --> 00:16:08,225
Step 4: X transpose e is 0, by line 2. So y hat transpose e is 0. The fitted values and the residuals are orthogonal.

75
00:16:08,675 --> 00:16:19,683
Part b: show that e transpose e equals y transpose y minus beta hat transpose X transpose X beta hat. Pause the video and try it.

76
00:16:20,133 --> 00:16:23,973
Step 2 of card E: write y as y hat plus e.

77
00:16:24,423 --> 00:16:37,330
Multiply out y transpose y. We get y hat transpose y hat, plus 2 y hat transpose e, plus e transpose e. The picture shows the three squared lengths.

78
00:16:37,780 --> 00:16:52,756
Step 4: by part a, y hat transpose e is 0. So the middle term goes away. The picture is a right triangle: the squared length of y is the squared length of y hat plus the squared length of e.

79
00:16:53,206 --> 00:16:56,875
Subtract y hat transpose y hat from both sides.

80
00:16:57,325 --> 00:17:00,717
Step 2 again: write y hat as X beta hat.

81
00:17:01,167 --> 00:17:08,527
Step 3: the transpose of X beta hat is beta hat transpose X transpose. This gives the result.

82
00:17:08,977 --> 00:17:33,767
A check with the satisfaction data: y transpose y is 187722. y hat transpose y hat is 183473.16. The difference is 4248.84. This is e transpose e, the same S S E as in part a of this video.

83
00:17:34,217 --> 00:17:41,598
Part c: show that a transpose X transpose e is 0 for any vector a. Pause the video and try it.

84
00:17:42,048 --> 00:17:58,155
Step 1 of card E: X transpose e is 0, from line 2 of part a. In the picture: the column of ones times e is the sum of the residuals, which is 0. Each covariate column times e is also 0.

85
00:17:58,605 --> 00:18:02,594
Group the product: a transpose, times X transpose e.

86
00:18:03,044 --> 00:18:09,935
Step 4: put in X transpose e equals 0. a transpose times the zero vector is 0.

87
00:18:10,385 --> 00:18:20,283
This holds for every a with one entry for each column of X. X a is a vector in the column space, and e is perpendicular to all of them.

88
00:18:20,733 --> 00:18:34,195
To recap part b. Card C gives a confidence interval: beta j hat plus or minus the t quantile times the standard error. If theta 0 is outside, the test rejects.

89
00:18:34,645 --> 00:18:45,802
Card D handles a linear combination: the weights, the estimate, the variance with each covariance two times, the standard error, and then the interval or the test.

90
00:18:46,252 --> 00:19:02,316
Card E proves identities: start from X transpose e equals 0, write y hat and e, move the transposes, and put in the zero. The next video uses these tools for fitted values, the mean response, and prediction.
